# Untitled

## Interfacial synthesis of hollow metal-organic framework capsules demonstrating selective permeability
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline materials that consist of metal ions and organic ligands linked together by coordination bonds. Because of their porosity and the possibility of combining large surface areas with pore characteristics that can be tailored, these solids show great promise for a wide range of applications. Although most applications currently under investigation are based on powdered solids, developing synthetic methods to prepare defect-free MOF layers will also enable applications based on selective permeation. Here, we demonstrate how the intrinsically hybrid nature of MOFs enables the self-completing growth of thin MOF layers. Moreover, these layers can be shaped as hollow capsules that demonstrate selective permeability directly related to the micropore size of the MOF crystallites forming the capsule wall. Such capsules effectively entrap guest species, and, in the future, could be applied in the development of selective microreactors containing molecular catalysts.
The interface between two immiscible liquids offers a scaffold for the controlled formation of thin layers with various compositions. Examples include the self-assembly and subsequent crosslinking of pre-formed nanosized objects<sup>1</sup> and direct reactive routes such as interfacial polymerization involving reagents dissolved in the different liquid phases<sup>2</sup>. A spherical liquid–liquid interface, obtained, for instance, by dispersing one solvent phase, as droplets, in the other, similarly facilitates the preparation of hollow shells<sup>3</sup>. For the direct synthesis of hollow capsules via this droplet-templating route, it is essential that the precursors are available in a form soluble either in the dispersed or continuous solvent phase, and that they react at the interface to yield the desired shell composition and structure. This strategy has previously been demonstrated in the preparation of both polymeric and inorganic capsules<sup>3,4</sup>. Because such capsules can be permeable to reagents, their application as microreactors has been proposed. However, it remains difficult to controllably incorporate uniform porosity at a molecular scale, a feature that would enable the fabrication of size-selective capsular microreactors.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are composed of metal centres bridged by organic ligands, resulting in a network with uniform pores of molecular dimensions. Because of this combination of inorganic and organic building units in their crystal lattices, MOFs are often referred to as hybrid framework compounds. The main driving force prompting exploration of these microporous solids is their large and uniform porosity<sup>5</sup>, which has potential in such diverse areas as gas storage<sup>6</sup>, catalysis<sup>7,8</sup> and adsorptive separation<sup>9,10</sup>. The same properties that make MOFs' interesting candidates in these areas are also highly desirable in applications that require selective permeation through uniform micropores, such as membrane separation, sensor technology and so on<sup>11,12</sup>. Key to their successful application in these fields is the availability of synthetic methods to prepare defect-free MOF layers; in other words, permeation should not occur as a result of
uncontrolled porosity through gaps or cracks, but should be conducted solely through the channels existing in the MOF crystal lattice.
A MOF crystal lattice is formed through the reaction of metal ions and linking ligands, which are introduced into a synthesis mixture as the inorganic and organic precursors, respectively. Because these precursors have markedly different solubility characteristics, it is not surprising that non-aqueous solvents and solvent mixtures are widely used to obtain a single-phase synthesis mixture that contains all reagents. In the preparation of MOFs as bulk powders, crystallites are typically formed throughout the synthesis mixture by homogeneous nucleation; subsequently, they are separated from the solution. Given the broad potential of thin MOF films, several methods have been proposed to induce MOF surface growth on a variety of support materials<sup>13–20</sup>. Heterogeneous nucleation on the support during direct in situ growth typically yields poor coverage<sup>13</sup>, so subjecting the support to a chemical pre-treatment to introduce additional functional groups onto the surface<sup>13–16</sup>, or performing a seeding step<sup>17–19</sup>, is a prerequisite to achieving continuous MOF coatings.
Here, we demonstrate how the difference in solubility characteristics of the organic and inorganic MOF precursors can be used in a synthetic approach that enables the preparation of uniform thin MOF layers through a self-completing growth mechanism. By using a biphasic synthesis mixture comprising two immiscible solvents, each containing one of the two MOF precursors, crystallization takes place only at the liquid–liquid interface. The approach of supplying reagents separately during synthesis was previously used in obtaining new crystal phases to avoid reagent degradation under harsh hydrothermal conditions<sup>21,22</sup> or to gain access to the single crystals used for structural characterization<sup>23,24</sup>. As described herein, it is, however, possible to use the interface between the immiscible liquids as the template for the formation of a MOF layer.
## Results and discussion
In all methods for synthesizing MOF films reported to date, the metal ions and ligand molecules applied to a film growing at a support–liquid interface have been supplied from the same direction. This has led to problems such as undesired nucleation in the solution or growth on top of previously formed crystals instead of on the support, resulting in incomplete support coverage and uneven membrane thickness (Fig. 1a). Such problems have had to be circumvented by enhancing the surface affinity of the support for one of the MOF precursors<sup>13–16</sup> or by implementing a separate seeding step<sup>17–19</sup>. A highly advantageous aspect of the interfacial growth presented here is that the ligands and metal ions approach the growing MOF film from opposite directions, allowing the growth to self-complete in just a single step (Fig. 1b). During the growth process, diffusion of the MOF precursors will be faster at the remaining defects than through the already formed layer, and new crystallites will be formed primarily at the defect sites, thereby sealing the gaps<sup>20</sup>. This same mechanism also causes the thickness of the film to be uniform. Moreover, as the interfacial growth is intrinsically self-completing, no surface modification of the support is necessary and the support can even be omitted entirely, resulting in free-standing membranes. Moreover, because such free-standing
membranes adopt the shape of the liquid–liquid interface during biphasic synthesis, this strategy also allows the resulting MOF layers to be shaped as three-dimensional objects.
