Similarly, dispersing the aqueous solution as droplets in a 1-octanol phase leads to the formation of hollow capsules, because the droplets, rather than a flat interface, serve as the template3. To obtain monodisperse droplets and to avoid mechanical stress on the capsules (for example, caused by stirring), droplets were generated by breaking off in a co-flowing stream, as illustrated in Fig. 226. The aqueous cupric acetate solution was introduced into the co-flowing organic ligand solution through a tapered capillary. Droplets detached from the capillary when the forces exerted by the co-flowing continuous phase exceeded the force due to interfacial tension. Subsequently, droplets travelled through the hydrophobic tubing, and the ligand molecules were able to react at the interface and form the capsule wall.
Given the continuous-flow nature of capsule synthesis, control over crystallization kinetics is essential to keep tubing to a practical length. To avoid a reduction in crystallization rate due to reactant depletion near the interface, the concentrations of both the ligand and metal salt solutions were close to maximum solubility in either phase ( \(70\;\mathrm{mM}\) and \(360\;\mathrm{mM}\), respectively). In addition, the selection of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) acetate as the metal ion source proved beneficial in decreasing the capsule preparation time when compared to \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) nitrate27. Further fine-tuning was achieved by adding a less than stoichiometric amount of an organic amine base \((175\;\mathrm{mM})\) to the organic phase, which accelerates MOF formation by deprotonation of the carboxylic acid groups of the ligand. Elemental analysis confirmed the absence of nitrogen compounds in the resulting \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) material.
