In the case of a defect-free membrane, molecules larger than the micropores of the \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) crystal lattice should be retained within the capsule. To test this, the water phase that templates
the interior of the droplets was spiked with Rose Bengal, a large dye molecule that neither disturbs the crystallization nor is incorporated in the micropores during crystallization. After collection of the capsules, the dye content in the surrounding solvent was determined by UV–vis spectroscopy following an incubation period at room temperature. The capsules were then broken by sonication to release the encapsulated dye molecules and the dye content was sampled again after centrifugation. Repeating this experiment for different incubation periods up to \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) proves the effective encapsulation of the dye, because the ratio of dye molecules inside and outside the capsules remains unaffected throughout the duration of the experiment (Fig. 4a). This result confirms the integrity of the capsule wall, because no defects large enough to allow the dye molecules to escape are present. The fraction of dye molecules effectively encapsulated also indicates that over \(90\%\) of the generated droplets give rise to such intact capsules. Retention of the dye was still complete when the experimental time was doubled. Only when the experiment duration was increased by a factor of 10 did we observe the formation of some loose separate crystallites that detached from the capsules. This was interpreted as being a result of Ostwald ripening\(^{36}\), which would begin at the smallest crystallites that form last during the growth of the capsule shell and thereby seal the remaining gaps in the membrane. Ostwald ripening could open up defects large enough for the dye molecules to escape. As the difference in diffusion coefficients of the dye and ethylene glycol in a solvent cannot account for the observed dye retention in the capsules\(^{34,37}\), the as-prepared capsules must have been completely free of even nanometre-sized cracks.
The results presented here illustrate a synthetic approach ideally suited to preparing layers of MOFs by taking advantage of the hybrid nature inherent to this class of materials. The difference in solubility characteristics of the organic and inorganic precursors enables the self-completing interfacial formation of a
MOF layer in a biphasic synthesis mixture, as the two precursors encounter each other from opposite sides of the liquid–liquid interface. As layer growth in this case does not depend on any specific surface functionalization of a support, the approach can be adapted to a wide range of porous support materials. Moreover, in the absence of a support, freestanding MOF layers are obtained that can be shaped as hollow capsules by droplet templating. The size-selective permeability displayed by such capsules, together with the mild synthesis and activation conditions that allow encapsulation of functional species during synthesis, makes these hollow structures interesting candidates for application as microreactors.
## Methods
Preparation of MOF films. In the case of \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\), the aqueous phase was prepared by adding \(7.2\,\mathrm{g}\) of cupric acetate monohydrate to \(100\,\mathrm{g}\) of a \(1{-}4\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) PVA (98% hydrolysed; \(M_{\mathrm{w}}\), 13,000–23,000) solution in water. After stirring for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) at room temperature, the cupric oxide particles formed in this solution were removed by centrifugation at \(3,000\;\mathrm{r.p.m.}\) (over \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) ). The resulting clear solution was used for the preparation of a thin \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) film by layering a solution of \(0.353\;\mathrm{g}\) of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid on top of \(20\;\mathrm{g}\) of 1-octanol. In the case of ZIF-8, a solution of \(60\;\mathrm{mg}\) of 2-methylimidazole in \(9\;\mathrm{g}\) of 1-octanol was layered on top of a solution of \(200\;\mathrm{mg}\) of zinc nitrate tetrahydrate in \(9\;\mathrm{g}\) of water. In both cases, the biphasic mixture was left to stand at room temperature.
Preparation of hollow \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) capsules. The aqueous phase used for the preparation of \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) capsules was prepared as described above. For some tests, up to \(20\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) ethanol was added. The organic ligand solution was prepared by dissolving \(0.353\;\mathrm{g}\) of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid in \(20\;\mathrm{g}\) of 1-octanol. This solution was heated to \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Hollow capsules of \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) were prepared immediately after adding \(0.8\;\mathrm{g}\) of triethylamine to this solution. The aqueous phase was injected through a hollow needle (23 gauge, cone tip: inner diameter, \(110\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\); outer diameter at the shaft, \(630\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\); outer diameter at the tip, \(420\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\)) in a co-flowing stream of the organic solution flowing through polytetrafluoroethylene tubing (inner diameter, \(0.76\;\mathrm{mm}\)). Both aqueous and organic solutions were supplied by syringe pumps at \(1.2\;\mathrm{ml}\;\mathrm{m}^{-1}\), respectively. Capsules prepared this way were collected in ethanol. The remaining 1-octanol was removed by repeatedly exchanging the supernatant with fresh ethanol.
Characterization. Scanning electron microscopy images were acquired using a Philips XL30 FEG instrument. For these measurements, capsules were first dried at \(60\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and then at \(120\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The capsules retained their spherical shape during drying. Powder XRD patterns of crushed capsules were recorded on a STOE STADI P Combi instrument in Debye Scherrer geometry \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}\;\mathrm{K}_{\alpha1}\right)\) using an IP position-sensitive detector \((2\theta\!=\!0{-}60^{\circ}\); \(\Delta2\theta\!=\!0.03^{\circ}\)). Nitrogen physisorption measurements were carried out using a Quantachrome Autosorb-1 apparatus after pretreatment at \(120\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum for \(12\;\mathrm{h}\). Thermogravimetric analysis was carried out on a TA instruments Q500 under oxygen flow. Samples were heated to \(350\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(2\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\). The absence of nitrogen compounds in the capsule wall was verified by performing elemental analysis using a CE Instruments EA-1110 CHN apparatus.
Selective permeation tests. To test the selective permeability of the capsule wall, \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) capsules were prepared as before with the inclusion of a probe molecule in the inner aqueous phase. In a first experiment, the large dye molecule Rose Bengal (4,5,6,7-tetrachloro-3',6'-dihydroxy-2',4',5',7'-tetraiodo-3H-spiro[isobenzofuran-1,9'-xanthen]-3-one disodium salt) was included as an additive. The dimensions of the Rose Bengal molecule were larger than the micropores in the \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right]\) crystal lattice. After collection in ethanol, the capsules were left standing at room temperature for a predetermined time. The dye content in the surrounding solvent due to leakage from broken capsules was then determined using a Perkin-Elmer Lambda 40 UV–vis spectrometer. Subsequently, all capsule walls were broken by sonication to release the remaining dye molecules from the capsule interior. The surrounding solvent was resampled to determine the increase in dye concentration after centrifugation (1 min, \(2,500\;\mathrm{r.p.m.}\)). This experiment was repeated for different incubation times. In a second experiment, the small molecule ethylene glycol \((5{-}10\;\mathrm{wt}\;\%)\) was included in the aqueous phase. The increase in concentration of ethylene glycol during collection (minute 0–40) and incubation (minute 40–200) was monitored by sampling the surrounding solvent and performing gas chromatographic analysis using a Shimadzu GC-2010 instrument. Analogously, the simultaneous diffusion of ethylene glycol and glycerol was tested by including both molecules \((5\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) each) in the aqueous phase.
